<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Vishnukundina dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">420 CE – 624 CE (approx.) · capital Denduluru</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Deccan dynasty of the Krishna-Godavari region that ruled coastal Andhra and Telangana before the rise of the Eastern Chalukyas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Vishnukundinas emerged in the 5th century, possibly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, and expanded towards the coast, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salankayana-dynasty">Salankayana dynasty</a>. Their capitals included Indrapalanagara, Amarapura and Denduluru. They became the main power of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> after the decline of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka dynasty</a>, with whom they had marriage ties.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhavavarma-ii">Madhavavarma II</a>, who performed horse sacrifices, presented themselves as Vedic rulers, though they also supported Buddhist monasteries. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate charters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> record land grants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>. The rock-cut cave temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/undavalli-caves">Undavalli</a> and Mogalrajapuram near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayawada">Vijayawada</a> are attributed to their period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 7th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> region and gave it to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubja-vishnuvardhana">Kubja Vishnuvardhana</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a> around 624.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vishnukundina-dynasty.jpg" alt="Vishnukundina dynasty" width="320" height="146"><figcaption>Image: Mitresh66 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VishnukundinCastCpr1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>420 CE – 624 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Denduluru</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Madhavavarma II, Vikramendravarma I, Govindavarma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vishnukundinas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=522&amp;focus=vishnukundina-dynasty" aria-label="Vishnukundina dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6836 1544 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vishnukundina dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7156 1800L7060 1568M7156 1800L7097 1795"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7060 1568h0M7097 1795h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7156 1800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7156 1800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=522&amp;focus=vishnukundina-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
